solution.text:
Given, initial frequency of light,
$$
f_{1}=4 \times 10^{14} \mathrm{s}^{-1}
$$
Final frequency of light, $f_{2}=5 \times 10^{14} \mathrm{s}^{-1}$
Change in wavelength $, \Delta \lambda=\lambda_{1}-\lambda_{2}$
or
$$
\Delta \lambda=\frac{c}{f_{1}}-\frac{c}{f_{2}}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{l}
=\frac{3 \times 10^{8}}{4 \times 10^{14}}-\frac{3 \times 10^{8}}{5 \times 10^{14}} \\
=\frac{3 \times 10^{8}}{10^{14}}\left(\frac{1}{4}-\frac{1}{5}\right) \\
=\frac{3 \times 10^{8}}{10^{14}} \times \frac{1}{20} \\
=1.5 \times 10^{-7} \mathrm{m}
\end{array}
$$
Now, we know that, Angular width of central maxima,
$$
\begin{array}{l}
\theta=\frac{2 \lambda}{d} \text { or } \Delta \theta=\frac{2 \Delta \lambda}{d} \\
d=\frac{2 \Delta \lambda}{\Delta \theta}
\end{array}
$$
Here, $\Delta \theta=0.6$ radian
$$
d=\frac{2 \times 1.5 \times 10^{-7}}{0.6}=5 \times 10^{-7} \mathrm{m}
$$
